onition-text mt-2.5 text-base leading-normal tracking-extra-tight text-gray-new-20 dark:text-gray-new-85 [\u0026_a]:rounded-sm [\u0026_li]:text-base [\u0026_li]:leading-snug [\u0026_li]:tracking-extra-tight [\u0026_ul]:my-0 [\u0026_ol]:my-0\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"This is a cross-post of an engineering blog that was originally \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://www.databricks.com/blog/how-lakebase-architecture-stays-resilient-cloud-failures\",\"children\":\"published on Databricks\"}],\". Neon and Databricks Lakebase both run on the same technology, and this engineering work benefits customers of both platforms.\"]}]}]]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"In the last year, agents have strained the limits of cloud infrastructure with new usage patterns:\"}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"ul\",null,{\"children\":[\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"children\":[\"Higher throughput of control-plane operations: Agents programmatically create and manage databases, storage, compute, and other infrastructure components at rates much higher than humans. In Neon, \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://www.databricks.com/blog/how-agentic-software-development-will-change-databases\",\"children\":\"agents create 4x as many databases as humans do\"}],\".\"]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"children\":[\"More demand for on-demand: Serverless, \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/autoscaling\",\"children\":\"autoscaling\"}],\", and auto-suspend infrastructure is the new norm. If the agent goes to sleep, why pay for provisioned infrastructure?\"]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"children\":[\"Capacity crunch: Demand for compute, GPUs, and cloud infrastructure \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://www.gartner.com/en/newsroom/press-releases/2026-04-22-gartner-forecasts-worldwide-it-spending-to-grow-13-point-5-percent-in-2026-totaling-6-point-31-trillion-dollars\",\"children\":\"is going up\"}],\". The notion of cloud having \\\"infinite\\\" capacity is showing cracks.\"]}],\"\\n\"]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"This is challenging for both platform builders and cloud providers. Control planes are seeing significant increases in request volume for creating, managing, and scaling infrastructure, stressing reliability. Allocating new cloud capacity won't always succeed. At the same time, agentic workloads demand data-plane-level reliability for core control-plane operations as part of their operational flows. In the last few months, we've seen agents drive an exponential increase in database starts, and now we are starting tens of millions of databases every day.\"}],\"\\n\",\"$L60\",\"\\n\",\"$L61\",\"\\n\",\"$L62\",\"\\n\",\"$L63\",\"\\n\",\"$L64\",\"\\n\",\"$L65\",\"\\n\",\"$L66\",\"\\n\",\"$L67\",\"\\n\",\"$L68\",\"\\n\",\"$L69\",\"\\n\",\"$L6a\",\"\\n\",\"$L6b\",\"\\n\",\"$L6c\",\"\\n\",\"$L6d\",\"\\n\",\"$L6e\",\"\\n\",\"$L6f\",\"\\n\",\"$L70\",\"\\n\",\"$L71\",\"\\n\",\"$L72\",\"\\n\",\"$L73\",\"\\n\",\"$L74\",\"\\n\",\"$L75\",\"\\n\",\"$L76\",\"\\n\",\"$L77\",\"\\n\",\"$L78\",\"\\n\",\"$L79\",\"\\n\",\"$L7a\",\"\\n\",\"$L7b\",\"\\n\",\"$L7c\",\"\\n\",\"$L7d\",\"\\n\",\"$L7e\",\"\\n\",\"$L7f\",\"\\n\",\"$L80\",\"\\n\",\"$L81\",\"\\n\",\"$L82\",\"\\n\",\"$L83\",\"\\n\",\"$L84\",\"\\n\",\"$L85\"]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,":HL[\"https://cdn.neonapi.io/public/images/pages/blog/resilience-to-failures-in-agentic-era/compute-session-count-by-lifetime.png\",\"image\"]\n:HL[\"https://cdn.neonapi.io/public/images/pages/blog/resilience-to-failures-in-agentic-era/bar%20charts-1-dark.jpg\",\"image\"]\n60:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"The resulting spate of failures and incidents amongst cloud services has taught us lessons that inform our reliability roadmap, and we want to share how we're making Neon's lakebase architecture and design more resilient to cloud failures. Some items are already in production, others are in flight.\"}]\n61:[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"id\":\"high-availability-architecture\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-tighter\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"High availability architecture\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#high-availability-architecture\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to High availability architecture\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3.5\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"path\",null,{\"stroke\":\"currentColor\",\"strokeLinecap\":\"square\",\"strokeLinejoin\":\"round\",\"strokeWidth\":1.4,\"d\":\"m3.938 12.688 1.75-11.375M8.313 12.688l1.75-11.375M3.063 4.813h8.75M2.188 9.188h8.75\"}]}]}]]}]\n62:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"At the foundation is our \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://www.databricks.com/blog/what-is-a-lakebase\",\"children\":\"separated compute and storage architecture\"}],\", where High Availability (HA) is a core design tenet of the system and not an add-on.\"]}]\n63:[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"id\":\"stateless-postgres-compute\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-extra-tight\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"Stateless Postgres compute\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#stateless-postgres-compute\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to Stateless Postgres compute\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3\",\"children\":\"$61:props:children:1:props:children:props:children\"}]}]]}]\n64:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"Unlike many cloud Postgres database service setups that are monolithic and have stateful compute, Postgres in \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://neon.com/docs/introduction/architecture-overview\",\"children\":\"Neon's lakebase architecture is stateless\"}],\". All durable data lives in a remote storage service, so the compute process holds no durable state on the local disk. If Postgres or the hardware it runs on fails, it can be instantly replaced without replicating data to a hot standby or running usual Postgres crash recovery. A hot standby in a monolithic setup requires a full copy of the data (not free), while crash recovery \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/wal-internals.html\",\"children\":\"must replay the write-ahead log\"}],\" from the last checkpoint, which scales with the write rate at the time of the crash and can take 10s of minutes, depending on configuration. Because the database contents are stored in our zone-resilient storage service, a single-compute Postgres instance in Neon has significantly improved availability compared to a single stateful Postgres instance, without the cost of an additional hot standby compute instance.\"]}]\n65:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"For databases that require the highest levels of availability, you can configure \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/high-av"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"ailability\",\"children\":\"high availability\"}],\" (currently available on  Lakebase). This provisions dedicated computes across multiple availability zones for your database, ensuring that your database remains available even if the cloud provider runs out of capacity during (or as a result of) the failure event. These computes can additionally be utilized to scale reads.\"]}]\n66:[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"id\":\"zone-redundant-storage-for-all\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-extra-tight\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"Zone redundant storage for all\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#zone-redundant-storage-for-all\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to Zone redundant storage for all\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3\",\"children\":\"$61:props:children:1:props:children:props:children\"}]}]]}]\n67:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"Monolithic Postgres setups are usually backed by local block devices that are rarely zone-redundant. This necessitates physical replication and costly hot standby replicas across multiple availability zones. In \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://neon.com/docs/introduction\",\"children\":\"Neon\"}],\", all databases, regardless of tier and configuration, are backed by \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://neon.com/storage\",\"children\":\"distributed, zone-redundant, highly available storage.\"}],\" Data is stored in highly durable, zone-redundant object storage, and performance is accelerated by NVMe SSD caches across multiple availability zones at no additional cost to you.\"]}]\n68:[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"id\":\"control-plane-is-the-new-data-plane\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-tighter\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"Control plane is the new data plane\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#control-plane-is-the-new-data-plane\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to Control plane is the new data plane\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3.5\",\"children\":\"$61:props:children:1:props:children:props:children\"}]}]]}]\n69:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"In monolithic cloud database service architecture, the data plane is the critical part of the service. It's designed for 99.99+% availability and \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-fault-isolation-boundaries/static-stability.html\",\"children\":\"static stability\"}],\". The control plane matters \\\"only\\\" for management operations. With agentic and on-demand workloads, the part of the control plane that starts databases is \",[\"$\",\"em\",null,{\"children\":\"effectively\"}],\" the data plane. This has changed how we think about our architecture. Currently, our control plane handles everything from starting databases to billing. The former is clearly more critical. We've had outages where background maintenance operations resource-starved on-demand database startups - that's clearly not ok.\"]}]\n6a:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"We're currently hard at work separating the critical parts of the control plane into a data plane controller service that handles only hot-path operations (start/suspend). This service has less business logic, a strict, minimal set of external dependencies (see the next section), and is engineered from the ground up with resilience, graceful degradation, and defense-in-depth top of mind.\"}]\n6b:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"To illustra"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"te how central the control plane is to database traffic, we can analyze compute session lifetimes (the time from auto-resume due to an incoming connection to shutdown due to inactivity). In Neon, 90% of compute sessions for auto-suspending databases are less than 10 minutes.\"}]\n6c:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"$\",\"$L5f\",null,{\"src\":\"https://cdn.neonapi.io/public/images/pages/blog/resilience-to-failures-in-agentic-era/compute-session-count-by-lifetime.png\",\"isDark\":true,\"children\":[\"$\",\"img\",null,{\"src\":\"https://cdn.neonapi.io/public/images/pages/blog/resilience-to-failures-in-agentic-era/compute-session-count-by-lifetime.png\",\"alt\":\"Neon compute sessions by lifetime\"}]}]}]\n6d:[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"id\":\"carefully-consider-critical-path-dependencies-including-cloud-provider-control-planes\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-tighter\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"Carefully consider critical path dependencies, including cloud provider control planes\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#carefully-consider-critical-path-dependencies-including-cloud-provider-control-planes\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to Carefully consider critical path dependencies, including cloud provider control planes\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3.5\",\"children\":\"$61:props:children:1:props:children:props:children\"}]}]]}]\n6e:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"Serving agentic workloads means creating and resuming databases must be highly reliable. Reliability is strongly correlated with the dependency chain and the amount of machinery involved in the flow. In a traditional setup with Postgres in cloud provider VMs, this goes well beyond the data plane:\"}]\n6f:[\"$\",\"ul\",null,{\"children\":[\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"children\":\"Cloud provider's compute control plane to provision VMs\"}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"children\":\"Available VM capacity (where the cloud provider controls the policy of who gets it)\"}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"children\":\"Cloud provider's block store control plane to provision local storage\"}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"children\":\"Cloud provider's networking control plane to allocate IPs, configure firewalls and network routes to the new VM\"}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"children\":\"If using Kubernetes (K8s) - an additional dependency on the K8s system services.\"}],\"\\n\"]}]\n70:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"In Neon, we take a different approach that drastically reduces the amount of control plane machinery involved in critical database flows:\"}]\n71:[\"$\",\"ul\",null,{\"children\":[\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"children\":\"We allocate a pool of big (often bare metal) instances from the cloud provider. We carry buffers to sustain cloud provider provisioning outages.\"}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"children\":[\"We built our \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://neon.com/docs/introduction/autoscaling-architecture\",\"children\":\"own vertically autoscaling virtualization layer\"}],\" that schedules multiple Postgres instances onto those cloud instances.\"]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"children\":\"We don't rely on cloud block store devices, but instead store data in our own zone-resilient storage that is ultimately backed in object stores like S3 or Azure Blob storage.\"}],\"\\n\"]}]\n72:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"Many other services at Databricks experience the same reliability challenges. This is where Neon benefits from being part of Databricks: Databricks has the means and is investing heavily in building a common platform to increase the reliability of all products on all three major clouds.\"}]\n73:[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"id\":\"compartmentalize-and-contain-the-blast-radius\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-tighter\",\"children\":[[\"$\","])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"Compartmentalize and contain the blast radius\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#compartmentalize-and-contain-the-blast-radius\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to Compartmentalize and contain the blast radius\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3.5\",\"children\":\"$61:props:children:1:props:children:props:children\"}]}]]}]\n74:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"Rather than running a single monolithic regional deployment, Neon composes a region from one or more identically shaped \",[\"$\",\"em\",null,{\"children\":\"cells\"}],\". A cell is a complete, self-contained slice of the Neon stack: Kubernetes, control plane, compute, and storage.\"]}]\n75:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"$\",\"$L5f\",null,{\"src\":\"https://cdn.neonapi.io/public/images/pages/blog/resilience-to-failures-in-agentic-era/bar%20charts-1-dark.jpg\",\"isDark\":true,\"children\":[\"$\",\"img\",null,{\"src\":\"https://cdn.neonapi.io/public/images/pages/blog/resilience-to-failures-in-agentic-era/bar%20charts-1-dark.jpg\",\"alt\":\"Neon cells architecture\"}]}]}]\n76:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"This helps in two ways:\"}]\n77:[\"$\",\"ul\",null,{\"children\":[\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"Scaling:\"}],\" To grow a region, we add another cell. When an existing Cell approaches scalability limits of Kubernetes and control plane, new project creation is routed to a freshly provisioned Cell. Cells are spun up quickly as demand grows.\"]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"Blast radius containment:\"}],\" Even with thorough testing and built-in protections, things still go wrong in production - Kubernetes control plane/system services trouble, code or config regressions, DoS situations etc. The cell boundary isolates faults and prevents the situation from spreading, leaving the other Cells in the region serving traffic normally.\"]}],\"\\n\"]}]\n78:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"Together, this enables our platform to scale a region elastically while limiting the blast radius of any single failure. During an \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://www.networkworld.com/article/4168878/aws-hit-by-us-east-1-outage-after-data-center-thermal-event.html\",\"children\":\"incident on May 8, 2026\"}],\", when AWS experienced issues with an Availability Zone in us-east-1, one of the cells had issues failing over to healthy nodes. The impact was contained to that cell. The other seven cells in the region failed over correctly, so the incident affected only ~13% of databases in the region. In this case, the cell-based architecture reduced the impact by roughly an order of magnitude.\"]}]\n79:[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"id\":\"failure-simulation-and-injection\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-tighter\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"Failure simulation and injection\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#failure-simulation-and-injection\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to Failure simulation and injection\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3.5\",\"children\":\"$61:props:children:1:props:children:props:children\"}]}]]}]\n7a:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"Redundancy architecture and principles aren't worth much unless they work in practice. One can brainstorm every possible failure mode, but \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Murphy%27s_law\",\"children\":\"Murphy's Law\"}],\" is alive and well, and complex systems always find a way to s"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"urprise you. Every Neon release goes through failure injection and chaos testing before it goes to production. We deploy the release to a real cluster, drive it with a mix of agentic and non-agentic OLTP and OLAP workloads at stress-level concurrency, and then start breaking things underneath. We kill processes, shoot down nodes, inject network failures, wipe disk contents, and restart components in loops, all while the workload keeps running. We use \",[\"$\",\"em\",null,{\"children\":\"failpoints\"}],\" liberally in our code to inject hard-to-reproduce errors, such as a crash at the worst possible time. This is driven by an internal fault-injection framework that can target a single process or coordinate cluster-wide faults across an entire cell.\"]}]\n7b:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"Our passing bar is stricter than \\\"the test didn't error\\\". We utilize open source tools like \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://github.com/sqlancer/sqlancer\",\"children\":\"SqlLancer\"}],\" and \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://github.com/sqlancer/sqlancer\",\"children\":\"SqlSmith\"}],\", along with similar internal tools, to verify correct Postgres behavior. While failure injection is running, we validate internal data consistency, that no committed transaction is lost, and that every component recovers to a consistent state on its own.\"]}]\n7c:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"We're now taking this one level up, from component-level chaos to whole-AZ down simulations. In a real cluster with workloads running, we programmatically disconnect an availability zone's network from the rest of the cluster and observe how the system reacts: how quickly storage shifts to surviving replicas, how fast computes are failed over to healthy AZs, how the proxy layer reroutes connections, and how long any individual database sees an outage. Our goal is that no workload should be down for more than 30 seconds.\"}]\n7d:[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"id\":\"measure-measure-measure\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-tighter\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"Measure, measure, measure\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#measure-measure-measure\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to Measure, measure, measure\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3.5\",\"children\":\"$61:props:children:1:props:children:props:children\"}]}]]}]\n7e:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"Lord Kelvin \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://archive.org/stream/popularlecturesa01kelvuoft#page/72\",\"children\":\"said\"}],\", \\\"\",[\"$\",\"em\",null,{\"children\":\"If you cannot measure it, it is not science\"}],\"\\\". We embody the same, and we make a science out of measuring availability and reliability. The user-visible status you see at \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://neonstatus.com/\",\"children\":\"https://neonstatus.com/\"}],\" is a high-level view. Internally, we measure Service Level Indicators (SLIs) and set targets (SLOs) for all system components and major operations, especially user-facing ones. For example, we measure:\"]}]\n7f:[\"$\",\"ul\",null,{\"children\":[\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"Database Availability:\"}],\" How many percent of the time every individual database is available. We don't just measure aggregate fleet availability, because an individual customer doesn't care if \",[\"$\",\"em\",null,{\"children\":\"the fleet\"}],\" had great availability if \",[\"$\",\"em\",null,{\"children\":\"their\"}],\" database was down.\"]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"Database Startup Time:\"}],\" How quickly a suspended database becomes available when you connect, or how quickly a brand new database starts up.\"]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"Database switchover/failover:\"}],\" Frequen"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"cy and latency. As infrequent as possible, and as quickly as possible when it does happen.\"]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"Storage:\"}],\" Availability and latency of page reads and durable writes from Postgres to storage. These tell us whether your workload gets what it needs.\"]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"Control Plane APIs:\"}],\" Success rates and latency of important operations such as branching.\"]}],\"\\n\"]}]\n80:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"Our goal is for every database to exceed 99.99% availability every month. We measure how close we are to that goal with \",[\"$\",\"em\",null,{\"children\":\"attainment\"}],\": How many % of the fleet's databases that met the goal. Below is Neon's availability attainment so far in 2026 for monthly active databases.\"]}]\n81:[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"table-wrapper\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"table\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"thead\",null,{\"children\":[\"$\",\"tr\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"th\",null,{\"style\":{\"textAlign\":\"left\"},\"children\":\"Month\"}],[\"$\",\"th\",null,{\"style\":{\"textAlign\":\"left\"},\"children\":\"Databases met 99.95%\"}],[\"$\",\"th\",null,{\"style\":{\"textAlign\":\"left\"},\"children\":\"Databases met 99.99%\"}]]}]}],[\"$\",\"tbody\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"tr\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"td\",null,{\"style\":{\"textAlign\":\"left\"},\"children\":\"2026-01\"}],[\"$\",\"td\",null,{\"style\":{\"textAlign\":\"left\"},\"children\":\"99.96%\"}],[\"$\",\"td\",null,{\"style\":{\"textAlign\":\"left\"},\"children\":\"99.85%\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"tr\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"td\",null,{\"style\":{\"textAlign\":\"left\"},\"children\":\"2026-02\"}],[\"$\",\"td\",null,{\"style\":{\"textAlign\":\"left\"},\"children\":\"99.95%\"}],[\"$\",\"td\",null,{\"style\":{\"textAlign\":\"left\"},\"children\":\"99.84%\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"tr\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"td\",null,{\"style\":{\"textAlign\":\"left\"},\"children\":\"2026-03\"}],[\"$\",\"td\",null,{\"style\":{\"textAlign\":\"left\"},\"children\":\"99.96%\"}],[\"$\",\"td\",null,{\"style\":{\"textAlign\":\"left\"},\"children\":\"99.81%\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"tr\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"td\",null,{\"style\":{\"textAlign\":\"left\"},\"children\":\"2026-04\"}],[\"$\",\"td\",null,{\"style\":{\"textAlign\":\"left\"},\"children\":\"99.93%\"}],[\"$\",\"td\",null,{\"style\":{\"textAlign\":\"left\"},\"children\":\"99.75%\"}]]}]]}]]}]}]\n82:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"Best-in-class reliability and availability are of utmost importance in operational systems. We're hard at work building your trust in our database service.\"}]\n83:[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"id\":\"the-team\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-tighter\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"The Team\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#the-team\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to The Team\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3.5\",\"children\":\"$61:props:children:1:props:children:props:children\"}]}]]}]\n84:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"The reliability work above is being driven by people who have spent careers building and operating relational databases. A few of them:\"}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"85:[\"$\",\"ul\",null,{\"children\":[\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"Jasraj Dange\"}],\" - Engineering leader on Lakebase and Neon, previously led work on Azure SQL Database Performance, Scalability and making Azure SQL Database a robust platform for Applications.\"]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"Hans Norheim\"}],\" - Focused on Lakebase and Neon availability \u0026 reliability, spent 13 years at Microsoft on SQL Server and Azure SQL Database, including the hot patching technology that lets SQL Server be updated without downtime, and the upgrade orchestration that holds Azure SQL Database to its 99.995% uptime SLA.\"]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"Stas Kelvich\"}],\" - Continuing to work on Lakebase and Neon after co-founding the company. Before Neon, he worked on Postgres internals at Postgres Professional for five years, including fault-tolerant multi-master replication with quorum commit, cross-node snapshot isolation using loosely synchronized clocks, and improvements to two-phase commit and logical replication.\"]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"John Spray\"}],\" - Leading Lakebase and Neon Storage. Previously led storage and compute driving key improvements for scale like sharding. Before that, distributed storage and systems work at Redpanda, Red Hat (Ceph), and Intel.\"]}],\"\\n\"]}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"3a:[[\"$\",\"meta\",\"0\",{\"charSet\":\"utf-8\"}],[\"$\",\"meta\",\"1\",{\"name\":\"viewport\",\"content\":\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover\"}]]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"86:I[27201,[\"/_next/static/chunks/0-0o8zjuqab7b.js?dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7\",\"/_next/static/chunks/0-wyrs755k4-p.js?dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7\",\"/_next/static/chunks/0i.l9589uvx0j.js?dpl=dpl_3oQTwA9tDsjxX7vtodZE2SLAZ1X7\"],\"IconMark\"]\n37:null\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"3c:[[\"$\",\"title\",\"0\",{\"children\":\"How Neon's lakebase architecture stays resilient to cloud failures\"}],[\"$\",\"meta\",\"1\",{\"name\":\"description\",\"content\":\"How Neon is engineered to withstand cloud failures in the age of agentic workloads, with HA architecture, cell-based isolation, and minimal critical-path dependencies.\"}],[\"$\",\"link\",\"2\",{\"rel\":\"manifest\",\"href\":\"/manifest.json\",\"crossOrigin\":\"$undefined\"}],[\"$\",\"meta\",\"3\",{\"name\":\"keywords\",\"content\":\"\"}],[\"$\",\"meta\",\"4\",{\"name\":\"category\",\"content\":\"Engineering\"}],[\"$\",\"link\",\"5\",{\"rel\":\"canonical\",\"href\":\"https://neon.com/blog/resilience-to-failures-in-agentic-era\"}],[\"$\",\"meta\",\"6\",{\"property\":\"og:title\",\"content\":\"How Neon's lakebase architect